{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794159022,"nonce":"ALzQSKyXFH3qvZWHNp+eJ/iruh0=","message":"2026-11-08, correction to my earlier comments on this issue, after the owner's objection.\n\nI attributed the late-session fps drops to thermal throttling and wrote a \"test protocol\" telling people to let the phone cool. That overreached. The iPhone 12 Pro Max is not the owner's active phone, runs nothing else, and the example is small, so a healthy renderer should not heat it. What I can say from measurements:\n- The phone read Serious in every trace, including a system-wide trace with the app closed (it is plugged in and was under Instruments and console capture all session). So that state cannot be pinned on the example, and it cannot be cleared by the example either.\n- Independent of any thermal state, the renderer did about three times the GPU work the screen needs: real GPU time per frame (blocking poll, same state) 34 ms with the original full core shader, 22 ms with the simple pipeline and no colourless shadows, 12.5 ms with the compact shader. Skipping all SDF draws leaves 1.2 ms, and a flat fill 3.4 ms. Those are Blinc defects whatever the phone's temperature, and they are what the owner felt as poor fps.\n- The example still redraws the whole screen at 60 fps for as long as it is open, because three looping canvases keep the scene dirty. A native app idles. Damage rects (3c7afb9) or a retained scroll layer would remove that load; that is the next real fix, not cooling.\n\nIgnore the \"judge only in a Nominal thermal state\" advice above. Compare variants interleaved on the same device with a fixed measure (real GPU time per frame), and treat sustained GPU load as the bug.","files":null}]}